COMMAND NAME: mppcrondump.sh

A wrapper script for gp_dump, which can be called from a crontab entry.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mppcrondump.sh -x <database_name> [-B <parallel_processes>] 
[-R <post_dump_script>] [-s <schema_name>] [-d <master_data_directory>] 
[-p | -m] [-c | -o] [-z] [-r] [-f <free_space_percent>] [-b] [-i] 
[-j] [-k] [-g] {[-t] [-C]} [-a] [-q] [-l <logfile_directory>] [-D] [-? | -v]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

mppcrondump.sh is a wrapper script for gp_dump. It allows you to 
schedule routine backups of a Greenplum database using cron (a 
scheduling utility for Unix operating systems). Cron jobs that 
call mppcrondump.sh should be scheduled on the master host.

Here is an example of the crontab file, which schedules a nightly dump 
of the customers database at one minute past midnight. Note that the 
SHELL is set to /bin/bash and the PATH includes the location of the 
Greenplum Database management scripts  this is required to 
run mppcrondump.sh. 

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:$MPPHOME/gpMgmt/bin
MAILTO=gpadmin
HOME=/home/gpadmin

# Greenplum Database nightly database dump job
01 0 * * * gpadmin mppcrondump.sh -x customers -a -q >> 
mpp_custdump.log


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-x <database_name>

Required. The name of the Greenplum database to dump.


-B <parallel_processes>

The number of segments to check in parallel for pre/post-dump validation. 
If not specified, the script will start up to 60 parallel processes 
depending on how many segment instances it needs to dump.


-R <post_dump_script>

The absolute path of a script to run after a successful dump operation. 
For example, you might want a script that moves completed dump files 
to a backup host.


-s <schema_name>

Optional. Dump only the named schema in the named database.


-d <master_data_directory>

Optional. The master host data directory. If not specified, the value 
set for $MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-p (primary segments only)

Optional. Dump the primary segment instances only. The default is to 
dump both primary and mirror segment instances. If a primary is down, 
the dump will reconfigure itself and dump the active mirror segment 
for the downed primary.


-m (mirror segments only)

Optional. Dump the mirror segment instances only. The default is to dump 
both primary and mirror segment instances. If a mirror is down, the dump will 
reconfigure itself and dump the active primary segment for the downed mirror.


-c (clear old dump files first)

Clear out old dump files before doing the dump. The default is not to 
clear out old dump files. This will remove the oldest dump directory except 
not the current dates dump directory. All dump sets within that directory 
will be removed.


-o (clear old dump files only)

Clear out old dump files only, but do not run a dump. This will remove the 
oldest dump directory except not the current dates dump directory. All 
dump sets within that directory will be removed.


-z (no compression)

Do not use compression. Default is to compress the dump files.


-r (no rollback on failure)

Do not rollback the dump files (delete a partial dump) if a failure is 
detected. The default is to rollback (delete partial dump files).


-f <free_space_percent>

Percentage of free disk space to reserve when calculating the available 
free disk space for the dump operation. The default is 10.


-b (bypass disk space check)

Bypass disk space check. The default is to check for available disk space.


-i (ignore parameter check)

Ignore the initial parameter check phase.


-j (vacuum before dump)

Run VACUUM before the dump starts.


-k (vacuum after dump)

Run VACUUM after the dump has completed sucessfully.


-g (copy config files)

Secure a copy of the master host configuration files postgresql.conf, 
pg_ident.conf, and pg_hba.conf.


-t (dump catalog)

Use pg_dump to dump the pg_catalog schema of the master (system catalog 
tables). Schema is dumped to db_dumps/<date>/<dbname>_pg_catalog_<date>.


-C (clean old catalog dumps)

Clean out old catalog schema dump files prior to create.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-q (no screen output)

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, but 
is still written to the log file.


-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets logging level to debug.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

A crontab file that runs a backup of the sales database nightly at one past midnight:

SHELL=/bin/bash

PATH=/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:$MPPHOME/gpMgmt/bin

01 0 * * * gpadmin mppcrondump.sh -x customers -a -q >> mpp_custdump.log


